---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const leads = assertion('the-county-leads-ohio-in-what-it-releases')
const roll = assertion('a-fifth-of-the-fall-is-the-list-changing')
const streams = assertion('what-goes-to-the-air-fell-and-the-wells-did-not')
const badday = assertion('what-ended-was-the-bad-day')

const spec = (a: typeof leads, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const leadsSpec = spec(leads, 'Million pounds, 2024')
const rollSpec = spec(roll, 'Per cent fall, 1987–2024')
const streamsSpec = spec(streams, 'Per cent fall, 1987–2024')
const baddaySpec = spec(badday, '90th percentile AQI')
---

<Article slug="first-in-ohio-and-the-air-is-clean">
  <p>
    Twenty industrial facilities in Allen County filed with the federal toxics inventory for 2024,
    and seventeen of them reported a quantity released. Added up, it is more than any other county
    in Ohio reported — more than Cuyahoga, which had a hundred and twenty-two reporting facilities
    and thirteen times the population. The county holds under one per cent of Ohio's people and
    eighteen per cent of what Ohio reported putting out.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={leads} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="counties"
    title="The three largest county totals in Ohio, 2024"
    caption="On-site releases in millions of pounds. Allen is first on 9.4 million from 17 reporting facilities; Cuyahoga, third, reports 4.7 million from 122."
    columns={['County', 'Million pounds']}
    rows={leads.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="counties" set:html={JSON.stringify(leadsSpec)} is:inline />
  <Gloss>
    The inventory is not a measurement. A facility over a reporting threshold computes what it
    released and files the number; nobody weighs it. The early years show the precision in the
    figures themselves — 43,000,000 pounds, 11,000,000, 4,400,000 — round to two significant
    figures because that is what the estimate was worth.
  </Gloss>
  <p>
    Two thirds of the county's total went down a deep injection well, into a confined formation
    below the drinking-water zone. That is a disposal method only one other county in Ohio used at
    all in 2024. Set the wells aside and the county is fifth rather than first, which is a different
    sentence about the same year and is also true.
  </p>

  <h2>The fall is real and smaller than the file first says</h2>
  <p>
    Over thirty-eight years the reported total drops from 69.8 million pounds to 9.4 — a fall of
    86.5 per cent, and the most quotable number in the file. It is also not a comparison. What must
    be reported is a list of chemicals with joining and leaving dates, and ammonium sulfate solution
    was on that list for the first years of the series and is not on it now. This county reported
    126 million pounds of it in three years.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={roll} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="roll"
    title="The same fall, three ways"
    caption="Restricted to the 85 chemicals reportable in every year of the series, the fall is 64.2 per cent rather than 86.5. Air releases need no such repair, because what left the list had gone down the wells."
    columns={['Measure', 'Per cent fall']}
    rows={roll.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="roll" set:html={JSON.stringify(rollSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>The air improved and the wells did not</h2>
  <p>
    Split by where the material went, the two streams have had different histories. Carcinogenic
    releases to the air are down more than nine tenths. Carcinogenic releases into the injection
    wells are almost exactly where they were in 1987, and have been flat for twenty years. The
    chimney and the wellhead are not the same story and the county total tells neither.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={streams} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="streams"
    title="Carcinogens, by where they went"
    caption="Releases of chemicals the inventory flags as carcinogens, 1987 against 2024. To the air: 546,216 pounds down to 45,369. To the injection wells: 2,160,000 down to 1,947,066."
    columns={['Stream', 'Per cent fall']}
    rows={streams.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="streams" set:html={JSON.stringify(streamsSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The air stream also changed hands completely. The nitriles plant on the county's east side put
    8.3 million pounds into the air in 1987, seven eighths of the county's total, and 77,227 pounds
    in 2024 — a cut of ninety-nine per cent. The largest emitter now is a fertiliser works, and
    what it emits is mostly ammonia.
  </p>

  <h2>What the monitors measured</h2>
  <p>
    Beside all of that sits a second record, and it is measurement rather than estimate: forty-five
    unbroken years of daily air-quality readings over this county. They improved, and they improved
    in a particular way. The middle of the distribution has barely moved in forty years. The top of
    it collapsed.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={badday} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="aqi"
    title="The 90th-percentile air quality index, five points in the record"
    caption="The day that only one in ten days is worse than. Lower is better; 100 is the threshold above which the air is unhealthy for sensitive groups."
    columns={['Year', '90th percentile AQI']}
    rows={badday.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="aqi" set:html={JSON.stringify(baddaySpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    In 1998 forty-one days over Allen County were unhealthy for somebody. In four of the last six
    years, none were. That is the whole of the improvement — the bad day ended, and the ordinary
    day was always ordinary.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is tempting to join the two records, and the arithmetic will not carry it. What sets this
    county's index is ozone and fine particles, which drift in from a region far larger than the
    county and are mostly not on the inventory's list at all; what is on the list is ammonia,
    acetonitrile and propylene. Both series improve across the same decades. Neither is offered
    here as the reason for the other.
  </p>
</Article>
